with d(verse, title, meaning, application, poster) as (values
  (1, $q$Dhṛtarāṣṭra's question$q$, $q$Blind king Dhṛtarāṣṭra asks Sañjaya what his sons and the Pāṇḍavas did when they gathered, ready to fight, on the field of Kurukṣetra, the field of dharma.$q$, $q$Every great decision begins with a question. Notice what you ask when you are anxious about an outcome you cannot see.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-05-cinematic.jpg$q$),
  (2, $q$Duryodhana goes to his teacher$q$, $q$Sañjaya reports that when Duryodhana saw the Pāṇḍava army drawn up in formation, he went to his teacher Droṇa and spoke to him.$q$, $q$Under pressure we often go straight to an authority to be reassured. Ask whether you want advice or only comfort.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-07-cinematic.jpg$q$),
  (3, $q$Counting the other side's strength$q$, $q$Duryodhana points out the great Pāṇḍava army, arranged by Droṇa's own gifted student, the son of Drupada.$q$, $q$Comparing yourself with rivals can sharpen you or only feed anxiety. Use it to prepare, not to stew.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-21-miniature.jpg$q$),
  (4, $q$Naming the heroes across the field$q$, $q$He lists brave archers in the Pāṇḍava ranks, such as Yuyudhāna, Virāṭa and Drupada, who match Bhīma and Arjuna in battle.$q$, $q$Fear often shows up as a list of other people's strengths. Write the list, then write your own.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-28-miniature.jpg$q$),
  (5, $q$More warriors named$q$, $q$Duryodhana goes on naming mighty fighters on the other side: Dhṛṣṭaketu, Cekitāna, the king of Kāśī, Purujit, Kuntibhoja and Śaibya.$q$, $q$Naming a worry makes it smaller. Vague dread is heavier than a clear list.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-34-silhouette.jpg$q$),
  (6, $q$Heroes and sons of heroes$q$, $q$He adds Yudhāmanyu, Uttamaujā, Subhadrā's son and the sons of Draupadī, all described as great chariot warriors.$q$, $q$Strength can come from a whole circle of people. Take stock of who stands with you.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-08-cinematic.jpg$q$),
  (7, $q$Now our own side$q$, $q$Duryodhana turns to his own army and tells Droṇa he will name the leaders worth knowing, for Droṇa's information.$q$, $q$After studying the opposition, take the same honest look at your own resources.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-29-miniature.jpg$q$),
  (8, $q$Leaders on his side$q$, $q$He names Droṇa himself, Bhīṣma, Karṇa, Kṛpa, Aśvatthāmā, Vikarṇa and the son of Somadatta, all known for winning battles.$q$, $q$Know your people and what each is good at before a big effort begins.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-03-cinematic.jpg$q$),
  (9, $q$Many who would give their lives$q$, $q$Many other heroes, armed with many weapons and skilled in war, are ready to give up their lives for Duryodhana.$q$, $q$Loyalty given to a flawed cause is still loyalty. Choose carefully whom and what you are loyal to.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-24-miniature.jpg$q$),
  (10, $q$Is our army enough?$q$, $q$Duryodhana assesses the two armies. The word he uses for his own, protected by Bhīṣma, can be read as 'limitless' or 'insufficient'; commentators read his mood both ways.$q$, $q$Confidence and doubt can sit in the same sentence. Listen to what your own words reveal.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-36-silhouette.jpg$q$),
  (11, $q$Protect Bhīṣma$q$, $q$He tells all his commanders to hold their positions and above all to protect Bhīṣma from every side.$q$, $q$Even in a plan with many parts, name the one thing that must not fail.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-01-cinematic.jpg$q$),
  (12, $q$Bhīṣma's lion roar$q$, $q$To cheer Duryodhana, the aged Bhīṣma roars like a lion and blows his conch loudly.$q$, $q$Encouragement can be given by a gesture as well as by words.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-26-miniature.jpg$q$),
  (13, $q$Conches and drums answer$q$, $q$Conches, kettledrums, tabors and horns suddenly sound together, and the noise is tumultuous.$q$, $q$A single act can set a whole crowd moving. Be mindful of what you set off.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-04-cinematic.jpg$q$),
  (14, $q$Kṛṣṇa and Arjuna blow their conches$q$, $q$Seated in a grand chariot drawn by white horses, Kṛṣṇa and Arjuna blow their divine conches.$q$, $q$Real readiness is calm and clear, not loud.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-09-cinematic.jpg$q$),
  (15, $q$Names of the conches$q$, $q$Kṛṣṇa blows Pāñcajanya, Arjuna blows Devadatta, and Bhīma of fearsome deeds blows the great conch Pauṇḍra.$q$, $q$Each person brings a distinct voice. Do not wish yours were someone else's.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-46-sculptural.jpg$q$),
  (16, $q$The other Pāṇḍavas sound their conches$q$, $q$King Yudhiṣṭhira blows Anantavijaya, and the twins Nakula and Sahadeva blow Sughoṣa and Maṇipuṣpaka.$q$, $q$A team works when each member is willing to be heard.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-37-silhouette.jpg$q$),
  (17, $q$Allies add their sound$q$, $q$The king of Kāśī, Śikhaṇḍī, Dhṛṣṭadyumna, Virāṭa and the unconquered Sātyaki also blow their conches.$q$, $q$Support often arrives from unexpected people once you take a stand.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-44-sculptural.jpg$q$),
  (18, $q$Every warrior blows his own conch$q$, $q$Drupada, the sons of Draupadī and the strong-armed son of Subhadrā each blow their own conches from every side.$q$, $q$Showing up fully, in your own way, is part of the effort.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-41-sculptural.jpg$q$),
  (19, $q$The sound shakes hearts$q$, $q$The mighty sound echoed through earth and sky and pierced the hearts of Dhṛtarāṣṭra's sons.$q$, $q$Resolve, even without a weapon, can unsettle those who rely on force.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-19-cosmic.jpg$q$),
  (20, $q$Arjuna looks at the army$q$, $q$Arjuna, whose banner bears Hanumān, sees Dhṛtarāṣṭra's men arrayed and, as the fighting is about to begin, lifts his bow and speaks to Kṛṣṇa.$q$, $q$There is a moment before any big step when you look up and truly see what you are walking into.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-16-cosmic.jpg$q$),
  (21, $q$Place my chariot between the armies$q$, $q$Arjuna asks Kṛṣṇa, the never-fallen, to drive his chariot into the space between the two armies.$q$, $q$Before you act, ask for a place to stand where you can see everything.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-23-miniature.jpg$q$),
  (22, $q$Let me see who I must fight$q$, $q$He wants to look at those who stand ready to fight, and to know with whom he must contend in this war.$q$, $q$It is wise to look squarely at what a decision will actually cost.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-38-silhouette.jpg$q$),
  (23, $q$Who has come to fight?$q$, $q$Arjuna wants to see those who are gathered to fight, eager to please the ill-minded son of Dhṛtarāṣṭra.$q$, $q$Ask whose purposes a conflict really serves.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-31-silhouette.jpg$q$),
  (24, $q$Kṛṣṇa draws the chariot forward$q$, $q$Sañjaya says that Kṛṣṇa, asked by Arjuna, drove the finest of chariots into the middle, between the two armies.$q$, $q$A good guide goes with you into the hard place instead of pointing from afar.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-40-silhouette.jpg$q$),
  (25, $q$Behold the Kurus$q$, $q$Facing Bhīṣma, Droṇa and all the kings, Kṛṣṇa says: 'Arjuna, look at these Kurus assembled.'$q$, $q$Sometimes the first step in change is simply to look, without turning away.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-48-sculptural.jpg$q$),
  (26, $q$Arjuna sees his own family$q$, $q$Arjuna sees fathers and grandfathers, teachers, uncles, brothers, sons, grandsons and friends standing in both armies.$q$, $q$Conflicts are rarely with strangers. They run through people we love.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-50-sculptural.jpg$q$),
  (27, $q$Relatives on both sides$q$, $q$He also sees fathers-in-law and well-wishers in both armies, and looks upon all his kinsmen standing there.$q$, $q$The hardest choices involve people we care for on every side.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-02-cinematic.jpg$q$),
  (28, $q$Overcome by compassion$q$, $q$Seized by deep compassion and sorrow, Arjuna says: 'Kṛṣṇa, seeing my own people gathered eager to fight...'$q$, $q$Compassion is not weakness. It is a signal that something matters deeply to you.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-10-cinematic.jpg$q$),
  (29, $q$Limbs fail, body trembles$q$, $q$He says his limbs are giving way, his mouth is dry, his body shakes and his hair stands on end.$q$, $q$The body speaks before the mind does. Notice anxiety in your body early and take it seriously.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-06-cinematic.jpg$q$),
  (30, $q$The bow slips from his hand$q$, $q$The bow Gāṇḍīva slips from his hand, his skin burns, he cannot stand, and his mind seems to be spinning.$q$, $q$When you are overwhelmed, pause and steady yourself before deciding anything.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-33-silhouette.jpg$q$),
  (31, $q$Bad omens, no good in it$q$, $q$Arjuna sees ill omens and cannot see any good coming from killing his own kin in battle.$q$, $q$Check whether you are reading signs or only fear, but do not ignore a deep sense that something is wrong.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-35-silhouette.jpg$q$),
  (32, $q$What use is victory?$q$, $q$He says he does not want victory, a kingdom or pleasures. What use is rule, enjoyment or even life itself?$q$, $q$Ask what you are actually working toward. Achievement without meaning feels empty.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-20-cosmic.jpg$q$),
  (33, $q$Those I would win it for are here$q$, $q$The very people for whose sake we want a kingdom, enjoyment and happiness stand here ready to give up their wealth and lives.$q$, $q$Success that costs the people it is meant for is not success.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-43-sculptural.jpg$q$),
  (34, $q$Teachers, elders, kinsmen$q$, $q$Teachers, fathers, sons, grandfathers, uncles, in-laws, grandsons and relatives stand before him.$q$, $q$Remember the people behind every role you are asked to fight in.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-25-miniature.jpg$q$),
  (35, $q$Not even for the three worlds$q$, $q$Arjuna says that even if they kill him, he does not wish to kill them, not for rule over the three worlds, let alone for the earth.$q$, $q$Know the point at which no reward is worth the price.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-47-sculptural.jpg$q$),
  (36, $q$Only sin from killing them$q$, $q$What joy could come from killing Dhṛtarāṣṭra's sons? Even though they are aggressors, killing them would bring only sin upon us.$q$, $q$Being in the right does not always make an action feel clean. Be honest about its weight.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-45-sculptural.jpg$q$),
  (37, $q$We must not harm our own$q$, $q$He says it is wrong to kill our own relatives, and asks how we could ever be happy after destroying our own people.$q$, $q$Think about how you will live with a victory after it is won.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-42-sculptural.jpg$q$),
  (38, $q$Greed blinds them$q$, $q$Even if those whose minds are overcome by greed see no fault in destroying a family or in betraying friends...$q$, $q$Other people's blindness is not a reason to share it.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-39-silhouette.jpg$q$),
  (39, $q$Should we not turn away?$q$, $q$...why should we, who clearly see the evil of destroying a family, not think of turning away from this sin?$q$, $q$When you can see the harm clearly, you carry the responsibility to act on it.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-15-cosmic.jpg$q$),
  (40, $q$When a family declines$q$, $q$When a family is destroyed, its long-held duties and traditions are lost, and with them, Arjuna fears, lawlessness overtakes the whole family.$q$, $q$Habits and shared traditions hold a community together. Notice what you are quietly letting erode.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-32-silhouette.jpg$q$),
  (41, $q$Social order breaks down$q$, $q$As lawlessness grows, Arjuna fears the family's moral and social order will be disturbed, causing confusion across generations.$q$, $q$Small breakdowns in trust and care compound across a whole group over time.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-22-miniature.jpg$q$),
  (42, $q$The chain of consequences$q$, $q$He says such confusion drags both the family and those who ruin it down, and that ancestors suffer when offerings and rites stop.$q$, $q$Our actions reach people we may never meet, including those who come after us.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-30-miniature.jpg$q$),
  (43, $q$Eternal duties are lost$q$, $q$By the faults of those who destroy a family and cause disorder, the age-old duties of family and community come to ruin.$q$, $q$Protect what has been passed down to you, such as values, skills and stories, before it is lost.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-12-cosmic.jpg$q$),
  (44, $q$What we have heard$q$, $q$Arjuna recalls hearing that people whose family duties are lost must live in suffering for a long time.$q$, $q$Fear of consequences can be a compass. It can also become a trap. Learn to tell the difference.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-27-miniature.jpg$q$),
  (45, $q$A great wrong for a kingdom$q$, $q$Alas, he says, we are set on doing a great wrong, ready to kill our own people out of greed for the pleasures of kingship.$q$, $q$Notice when ambition has quietly pulled you toward something you would never choose in a calm hour.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-49-sculptural.jpg$q$),
  (46, $q$Better to be struck down$q$, $q$He says it would be better for him if Dhṛtarāṣṭra's armed sons were to kill him, unarmed and not resisting, in battle.$q$, $q$Despair can make a harmful choice look noble. Speak with someone you trust before accepting such a thought.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-02-cinematic.jpg$q$),
  (47, $q$Arjuna sits down$q$, $q$Having said this on the battlefield, Arjuna throws down his bow and arrows and sits on the chariot seat, his mind overcome with grief.$q$, $q$Stopping is sometimes the first honest step. Gītā begins its teaching exactly here, with a person who admits he cannot go on.$q$, $q$https://zsauxjqrtdrjbpanlypq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/reels/backgrounds/geeta-bg-06-cinematic.jpg$q$)
